    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Medical Parasitology Examination</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Suez University • Faculty of Medicine • Respiratory Module</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part 1: Introduction and Classification of Lung Parasites</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">1. Which of the following protozoan parasites can cause a pulmonary abscess or cyst infection according to the official module list?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Amoebic lung abscess (Cyst)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Hydatid cyst</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Paragonimiasis</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Schistosomiasis</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">2. Which of the following respiratory-affecting organisms is taxonomically classified under Arthropods?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Ascariasis</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Hookworm</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> House dust mites</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Strongyloidiasis</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">3. Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia malayi, and Dirofilaria immitis share which of the following primary routes of transmission?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Ingestion of contaminated food</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Vector-borne transmission via insect bites</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Direct skin penetration</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Inhalation of suspended dust particles</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">4. Which group of human parasites shares ingestion as their common primary route of transmission?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Schistosoma mansoni, Hookworm, Strongyloides stercoralis</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Echinococcus granulosus, Paragonimus westermani, Ascaris lumbricoides</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia malayi, Brugia timori</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Sarcoptes scabiei, Demodex folliculorum</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">5. Which of the following clinical syndromes is NOT classified as a classic presentation of Parasitic Eosinophilic Lung Disease?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Loeffler's syndrome</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Tropical eosinophilia syndrome</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Visceral larva migrans</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Amoebic lung abscess</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">6. Based on protozoan taxonomy, which organelles are utilized by Amoebae for structural motility?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Cilia</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Pseudopodia</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Flagella</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Specialized polar filaments</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">7. Apicomplexa (Sporozoa) parasites are uniquely characterized by which structural definition?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Unicellular organisms moving exclusively by specialized cilia</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Multicellular unsegmented nematodes</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Tissue parasites that do not possess distinct organs of locomotion</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Segmented flatworms with hooks and suckers</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">8. Morphologically, adult Trematodes (Flukes) are generalistically described as:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Cylindrical, elongated, and unsegmented worms</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Leaf-like and completely unsegmented flatworms</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Tape-like, elongated, and highly segmented flatworms</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Microscopic unicellular structures with spined surfaces</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">9. What is the universally recognized common medical name for Paragonimus westermani?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> African Lung Fly</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Oriental Lung Fluke</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Giant Liver Fluke</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Intestinal Blood Fluke</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">
                ANSWER KEY (PAGE 1): Q1: A | Q2: C | Q3: B | Q4: B | Q5: D | Q6: B | Q7: C | Q8: B | Q9: B
            </div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <div>Suez University • Faculty of Medicine</div>
                <div>Page 1 of 4</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Medical Parasitology Examination</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Suez University • Faculty of Medicine • Respiratory Module</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part 2: Paragonimiasis Life Cycle and Morphology</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">10. What are the approximate structural physical dimensions of a mature adult Paragonimus westermani fluke?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 12 mm × 3 mm</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> 1 cm × 0.7 cm</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> 90 µm × 55 µm</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> 5 cm × 5 cm</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">11. The protective external body wall (cuticle) of a mature adult Paragonimus westermani contains:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Highly developed posterior muscular suckers only</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Numerous small spine-like structures (Spines)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Dynamic locomotive cilia bands</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Segmented rings without extensions</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">12. Which African countries are explicitly documented within the geographical distribution of Paragonimus westermani?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Egypt and Tunisia</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Nigeria and Cameroon</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> South Africa and Kenya</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Madagascar and Sudan</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">13. Which developmental morphological type of cercaria is found in the life cycle of the oriental lung fluke?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Leptocercous cercaria</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Furcocercous cercaria</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Microcercous cercaria (short, stumpy tail)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Gymnocephalus cercaria</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">14. Which organism serves as the obligate first intermediate host (1st IH) for Paragonimus westermani?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Marine crabs or crayfish</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Freshwater prawns</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Freshwater operculate snails of the genus Semisulcospira</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Domesticated mammals like pigs</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">15. What is the specific infective stage responsible for transmitting Paragonimiasis to human hosts?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Free-swimming miracidia in water bodies</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Microcercous cercariae directly penetrating skin</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Encysted metacercariae inside the tissues of freshwater crustaceans</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Embryonated eggs found in contaminated soil</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">16. Which group of mammals can naturally act as reservoir hosts (RH) for Paragonimus westermani?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Dogs, cats, pigs, and monkeys</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Sheep, goats, and cattle only</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Migratory birds and marine fish</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Commensal rats and field mice</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">17. Where do the adult worms of Paragonimus westermani typically reside to lay their eggs inside humans?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Lumen of the duodenum</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Fibrotic cystic cavities within the lungs</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Intrahepatic bile ducts of the liver</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Inferior mesenteric venules</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">18. Which of the following sets of parameters describes the diagnostic egg of Paragonimus westermani?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Translucent, thin-shelled, with a prominent lateral spine</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> 90×55 µm, brown, thick-shelled, operculated, containing an unsegmented embryo</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Barrel-shaped with prominent bipolar clear plugs</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Spherical with a thick radially striated shell containing an oncosphere</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">
                ANSWER KEY (PAGE 2): Q10: B | Q11: B | Q12: B | Q13: C | Q14: C | Q15: C | Q16: A | Q17: B | Q18: B
            </div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <div>Suez University • Faculty of Medicine</div>
                <div>Page 2 of 4</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

