        <div class="section-title">Part 3: Paragonimus Clinical Manifestations and Introduction to Loeffler's</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">19. Due to presentation overlapping, chronic Pulmonary Paragonimiasis is frequently misdiagnosed as:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Acute asthmatic bronchitis</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Pulmonary Tuberculosis (TB)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Chronic end-stage renal failure</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Periportal liver fibrosis</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">20. Ectopic migration of immature flukes into the central nervous system leads to Cerebral Paragonimiasis, clinically resulting in:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Acute liver failure and severe generalized ascites</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Epileptiform seizures, visual disturbances, and motor deficits</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Painful hematuria and terminal dysuria</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Permanent sensorineural hearing loss</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">21. To confirm a diagnosis in both early and chronic stages of Paragonimus westermani infection, which serological methodology is preferred?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Widal agglutination test</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> ELISA technique</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Sputum culturing on Lowenstein-Jensen medium</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Immediate patch skin testing</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">22. Chest radiographs (X-rays) and computed tomography (CT) scans of individuals with active pulmonary paragonimiasis typically reveal:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Nodular or ring shadows and cavities</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Isolated hyper-trophy of the left ventricle wall</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Diffuse calcification of the diaphragm muscles</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Complete collapse of the main pulmonary artery segment</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">23. Which medical scientist first formally characterized the pathology of Loeffler's Syndrome, and in what year?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Louis Pasteur in 1890</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Professor William Loeffler at Zurich University in 1932</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Dr. Ghada Salah in 2017</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Robert Koch in 1905</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">24. What is the precise medical definition of Loeffler's Syndrome?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Irreversible structural destruction of the pulmonary parenchyma caused by adult trematodes</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> A transient, self-limiting respiratory hypersensitivity response with pulmonary eosinophilic infiltration driven by migrating nematode larvae</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Fulminant bacterial lobar pneumonia localized to the inferior lobes</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> An amebic abscess collection located within the right pleural space</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">25. Loeffler's Syndrome is classically associated with peripheral blood eosinophilia showing percentage ranges between:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Less than 1% to 5%</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> From less than 10% to more than 60%</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Exactly 100% in all cases</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Absolute depletion down to 0%</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">26. Following infective larval penetration through human skin in Hookworm or Strongyloides infections, respiratory symptoms of Loeffler's present after:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 24 hours only</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> One week</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> 9 to 12 days</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Two full months</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">27. In the case of an Ascaris lumbricoides infection, respiratory signs of Loeffler's syndrome manifest how long after ingesting embryonated eggs?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 1 to 3 days</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> 9 to 12 days</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> 3 weeks</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> 6 months</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">
                ANSWER KEY (PAGE 3): Q19: B | Q20: B | Q21: B | Q22: A | Q23: B | Q24: B | Q25: B | Q26: B | Q27: B
            </div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <div>Suez University • Faculty of Medicine</div>
                <div>Page 3 of 4</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

